igure></div><p>And the gates control how that memory changes:</p><ul><li><p=
>=CE=B1 (alpha) regulates how much of the old memory to forget (decay).</p>=
</li><li><p><span>=CE=B2 (beta) regulates how much the current token at tim=
e step </span><em>t</em><span> updates the memory.</span></p></li></ul><p>(=
And the final output gate, not shown in the snippet above, is similar to ga=
ted attention; it controls how much of the output is kept.)</p><p>So, in a =
sense, this state update in Gated DeltaNet is similar to how recurrent neur=
al networks (RNNs) work. The advantage is that it scales linearly (via the =
for-loop) instead of quadratically with context length.</p><p>The downside =
of this recurrent state update is that, compared to regular (or gated) atte=
ntion, it sacrifices the global context modeling ability that comes from fu=
ll pairwise attention.</p><p><span>Gated DeltaNet, can, to some extend, sti=
ll capture context, but it has to go through the memory (</span><em>S</em><=
span>) bottleneck. That memory is a fixed size and thus more efficient, but=
 it compresses past context into a single hidden state similar to RNNs.</sp=
an></p><p>That=E2=80=99s why the Qwen3-Next and Kimi Linear architectures d=
on=E2=80=99t replace all attention layers with DeltaNet layers but use the =
3:1 ratio mentioned earlier.</p><h2 class=3D"header-anchor-post"><strong>2.=
7 DeltaNet Memory Savings</strong><div class=3D"pencraft pc-display-flex pc=
-alignItems-center pc-position-absolute pc-reset header-anchor-parent"><div=
