ton></div></div></h2><p>TRM refines its answer through two alternating upda=
tes:</p><ol><li><p>It computes a latent reasoning state from the current qu=
estion and answer.</p></li><li><p>It then updates the answer based on that =
latent state.</p></li></ol><p>The training runs for up to 16 refinement ste=
ps per batch. Each step performs several no-grad loops to iteratively refin=
e the answer. This is followed by a gradient loop that backpropagates throu=
gh the full reasoning sequence to update the model weights.</p><p>It=E2=80=
=99s important to note that TRM is not a language model operating on text. =
However, because (a) it=E2=80=99s a transformer-based architecture, (b) rea=
soning is now a central focus in LLM research, and this model represents a =
distinctly different take on reasoning, and (c) many readers have asked me =
to cover HRM (and TRM is its more advanced successor) I decided to include =
it here.</p><p>While TRM could be extended to textual question-answer tasks=
 in the future, TRM currently works on grid-based inputs and outputs. In ot=
her words, both the =E2=80=9Cquestion=E2=80=9D and the =E2=80=9Canswer=E2=
=80=9D are grids of discrete tokens (for example, 9=C3=979 Sudoku or 30=C3=
=9730 ARC/Maze puzzles), not text sequences.</p><h2 class=3D"header-anchor-=
post"><strong>5.2 How Does TRM Differ From HRM?</strong><div class=3D"pencr=
